Subject: [PATCH 13/13 RFC] media: meson: vdec: convert Meson8b capture output

Meson8b's canvas has no endian control, so the firmware writes every
64-bit word byte-reversed, while the capture buffers are also the
decoder's reference pictures. The word cannot be fixed up in place: a
later picture would then decode from swapped references.

Each capture buffer gets a private canvas block in the firmware's own
layout, and the picture is converted into the buffer the client dequeues
when that buffer is done. The block comes from dma_alloc_coherent() so the
canvas stays physically contiguous, and the CPU reads it through a cached
vmap() alias invalidated before each read, because reading the uncached
mapping costs more than the conversion itself. The client still sees
NV12M.

The conversion is scalar. A NEON body halves it at 1080p, and belongs in
its own translation unit with the kernel_neon_begin() brackets once this
path is known to work on the silicon.

Two limits move to fit the extra block in the 64 MiB of CMA these boards
have: twelve capture buffers instead of the GX twenty-four, and the 4 MiB
bitstream FIFO the vendor stack uses instead of 16 MiB.

This removes the STREAMON rejection that kept Meson8b from decoding at
all. Nothing here has run on hardware.

---
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/Kconfig b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/Kconfig
index 39627f9..25cecf6 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/Kconfig
@@ -10,5 +10,5 @@ config VIDEO_MESON_VDEC
 	help
 	Support for the video decoder found in gxbb/gxl/gxm chips,
 	with experimental platform bring-up for Meson8b (S805).
-	Meson8b streaming is disabled until capture byte-order conversion
-	is implemented. It requires its own firmware.
+	Meson8b needs its own firmware and converts the capture output in
+	software, because its canvas has no byte-order control.
